summary.text:
We need to write a function block (FB) in structured text (ST) for PLC. It should implement temperature logic with given interface: inputs TemperatureSensorInput REAL, SetTemperature REAL; outputs CurrentTemperature REAL, SetTempDisplay REAL, HeaterStatus BOOL, OverheatProtection BOOL, HeaterOutput BOOL.

Requirements: Read sensor input (input provided), compare with set temp, turn on heater if actual < set, turn off if actual > set. Overheat protection: if heater works continuously for more than 5s, trigger overheating protection, turn off heater, wait 3s before protection can be lifted. After that, presumably the heater can be turned on again if conditions met. Provide status. Sensor fault if input <0 or >100 -> turn off heater.

We need to implement timing. Use internal variables: timer for heater on duration, timer for cooling/wait period. Need to manage state: normal operation, overheating protection active, wait period after protection lifts. Also heater status output indicates whether heater is currently on (maybe whether it is commanded on, or actual output? Probably HeaterStatus = HeaterOutput). HeaterOutput is control output. OverheatProtection indicates if protection is triggered (active). 

Behavior details: 
- If sensor fault, turn off heater, but should still output current temperature? Probably CurrentTemperature = input even if fault? But requirement says display current temperature, maybe we can still display input. Also fault implies heater off.
- Overheat protection: if heater continuously on for more than 5s, trigger protection, turn off heater. Then must wait 3s before protection can be lifted. During that 3s, protection remains active (OverheatProtection = TRUE), heater off. After 3s, protection is lifted (OverheatProtection = FALSE) and normal control resumes (if temp still low, heater can turn on again). But note: the heater should not turn on during the wait period. So after protection triggered, we set a timer for 3s, and only after that timer expires do we allow normal control again. We also need to reset the continuous on timer when heater turns off for any reason (including protection). When protection triggers, we set OverheatProtection = TRUE, HeaterOutput = FALSE, and start cooling timer. After 3s, OverheatProtection = FALSE, and normal operation resumes.

Need to handle transitions: When heater is turned on (due to low temp and not protected), start a timer measuring continuous on time. If heater is turned off for any reason (temp reached setpoint, sensor fault, protection trigger), reset that on timer. However, if protection triggers, we set protection and then the heater off, and we should not restart the on timer until protection lifts and heater is on again. Also, if during protection, temp is still low, we must not turn on heater until protection is lifted. So normal control should be gated by OverheatProtection: if OverheatProtection is TRUE, do not turn on heater even if temp < set.

So algorithm:
- Always output CurrentTemperature = TemperatureSensorInput (or maybe if fault? keep same). SetTempDisplay = SetTemperature.
- Check sensor fault: if input < 0 or > 100, then HeaterOutput = FALSE, HeaterStatus = HeaterOutput, OverheatProtection =? Should we also reset timers? Fault condition should turn off heater and probably not trigger overheat protection, but we should maybe keep protection state? Requirements: "if the read temperature value ... is considered a sensor fault, and the heater should be turned off." It doesn't specify to trigger overheat protection. Likely protection should not be triggered by fault, and if protection is currently active, maybe fault does not affect it? But if sensor fault occurs, we cannot properly control temperature, so we turn off heater. We might still keep protection state as is, but if overheat was active, it remains active until its timer expires. However, if fault occurs and protection is not active, we just turn off heater and maybe reset continuous on timer because heater turned off. We should handle fault independently. We'll define a boolean SensorFault.

- Overheat protection state: a boolean OverheatProtectionActive (or use output variable). We'll use internal variable bOverheatProtection.

- Timers: Use TON (timer on-delay) for continuous on time. We need a timer to measure how long HeaterOutput has been TRUE. Alternatively, we can use an accumulating time variable that increments when heater is on and resets when off. But using standard TON is easier: TON with input = HeaterOutput and PT = T#5s, Q indicates elapsed >=5s. But TON's behavior: when input goes TRUE, it starts accumulating; if input goes FALSE, it resets output and ET to 0. So we can use that. However, we need to detect when it has been on for 5s continuously. So we can have a TON instance for heaterOnTimer. When HeaterOutput is TRUE, the timer runs. When it reaches 5s (Q becomes TRUE), that means continuous on for 5s, and we should trigger overheat protection. But we must be careful: once Q is TRUE, it will stay TRUE as long as input is TRUE and ET >= PT. So we can use rising edge of Q to trigger protection. Alternatively, we can check if Q is TRUE and not already in protection, then trigger protection. After triggering protection, we set HeaterOutput FALSE, which will reset the TON (input goes FALSE, Q goes FALSE, ET resets). That is fine. So we can use a TON instance: HeaterOnTimer(IN := HeaterOutput, PT := T#5s). Then when HeaterOnTimer.Q becomes TRUE (or is TRUE) and we are not already in protection, we set protection. But need to ensure we only trigger once per continuous on period. Use a rising edge trigger on Q.

- Cooling/wait timer: After protection triggered, we need to wait 3s. Use a TON for cooling timer: CoolingTimer(IN := OverheatProtection, PT := T#3s). When protection becomes TRUE, timer runs; after 3s, Q becomes TRUE, which indicates wait period over, so we can lift protection. So we need to detect when CoolingTimer.Q becomes TRUE, then set OverheatProtection = FALSE. But careful: If we set OverheatProtection = FALSE, the input to CoolingTimer goes FALSE, resetting its Q. So we need to handle that properly. We can use a rising edge on CoolingTimer.Q to set protection off. However, we also need to ensure that while protection is active, the timer is running. So we set OverheatProtection = TRUE when triggering, and then when CoolingTimer.Q goes TRUE, we set OverheatProtection = FALSE. That will reset CoolingTimer.

So pseudocode in ST:

VAR
    HeaterOnTimer: TON;
    CoolingTimer: TON;
    bOverheatProtection: BOOL := FALSE;
    bSensorFault: BOOL;
    // maybe use edge detectors
    rTrigHeaterOnQ: R_TRIG;
    rTrigCoolingQ: R_TRIG;
END_VAR

Implementation in FB body:

// Compute sensor fault
bSensorFault := (TemperatureSensorInput < 0.0) OR (TemperatureSensorInput > 100.0);

// Output display values
CurrentTemperature := TemperatureSensorInput;
SetTempDisplay := SetTemperature;

// Heater control logic
IF bSensorFault THEN
    HeaterOutput := FALSE;
    // Possibly we don't trigger overheat protection from fault, leave as is.
    // But if protection active, it continues cooling timer.
ELSE
    // Normal control, but only if not in overheat protection
    IF NOT bOverheatProtection THEN
        // Compare temperatures
        IF TemperatureSensorInput < SetTemperature THEN
            HeaterOutput := TRUE;
        ELSE
            HeaterOutput := FALSE;
        END_IF;
    ELSE
        // In overheat protection, heater must be off
        HeaterOutput := FALSE;
    END_IF;
END_IF;

// Manage overheat protection trigger
HeaterOnTimer(IN := HeaterOutput, PT := T#5s);
rTrigHeaterOnQ(CLK := HeaterOnTimer.Q);
IF rTrigHeaterOnQ.Q AND NOT bSensorFault THEN // maybe sensor fault should not trigger? Actually if heater is on, sensor fault could have happened? But if sensor fault, heater is off so timer won't run. So okay.
    bOverheatProtection := TRUE;
END_IF;

// Manage cooling timer / protection lift
CoolingTimer(IN := bOverheatProtection, PT := T#3s);
rTrigCoolingQ(CLK := CoolingTimer.Q);
IF rTrigCoolingQ.Q THEN
    bOverheatProtection := FALSE;
END_IF;

// Outputs
OverheatProtection := bOverheatProtection;
HeaterStatus := HeaterOutput;

But note: When we set bOverheatProtection := TRUE, HeaterOutput will be set FALSE in next scan because the ELSE branch condition sees bOverheatProtection = TRUE. However, we are setting it immediately within the same scan? In the normal control section, we check bOverheatProtection at that moment (before it's set maybe). The order of execution matters. We should ensure HeaterOutput is turned off when protection triggers. We can explicitly set HeaterOutput := FALSE when triggering protection, to be safe. But the ELSE branch handles it if bOverheatProtection is TRUE. However, if we trigger protection after the heater control section, then in the same scan HeaterOutput might still be TRUE for a short time. That could cause issues. Better to structure: first compute protection state, then heater control based on protection state.

Alternative: Use a state machine or careful ordering:

1. Compute sensor fault.
2. Update timers and protection state based on current HeaterOutput? But HeaterOutput should be determined after protection known. Circular dependency.

Better to split: 
- Determine if heater should be on based on temperature and not fault and not protection from previous scan.
- But protection trigger depends on how long heater has been on. So we need to evaluate HeaterOutput first, then update protection, then maybe override HeaterOutput if protection just triggered.

Approach:
- Set HeaterOutput based on temperature and fault and previous protection state, but if protection is already active, force off.
- Then run HeaterOnTimer with HeaterOutput. If timer Q rising edge, set bOverheatProtection := TRUE, and also force HeaterOutput := FALSE immediately (since protection just triggered).
- Then run CoolingTimer with bOverheatProtection. If its Q rising, set bOverheatProtection := FALSE.
- In the next scan, HeaterOutput will be computed again, now seeing bOverheatProtection FALSE (if cooling done), so normal operation resumes.

This works because we force HeaterOutput off immediately when protection triggers, avoiding delay. We just need to ensure that when protection triggers, we set HeaterOutput to FALSE. We can do that in the IF block for protection trigger.

So pseudocode:

// Sensor fault
bSensorFault := (TemperatureSensorInput < 0.0) OR (TemperatureSensorInput > 100.0);

// Outputs for display
CurrentTemperature := TemperatureSensorInput;
SetTempDisplay := SetTemperature;

// Heater control (initial, before protection update)
IF bSensorFault THEN
    HeaterOutput := FALSE;
ELSIF bOverheatProtection THEN
    HeaterOutput := FALSE;
ELSE
    IF TemperatureSensorInput < SetTemperature THEN
        HeaterOutput := TRUE;
    ELSE
        HeaterOutput := FALSE;
    END_IF;
END_IF;

// Manage overheat protection trigger
HeaterOnTimer(IN := HeaterOutput, PT := T#5s);
rTrigHeaterOnQ(CLK := HeaterOnTimer.Q);
IF rTrigHeaterOnQ.Q THEN
    // Heater has been on continuously for 5s, trigger protection
    bOverheatProtection := TRUE;
    HeaterOutput := FALSE; // immediately turn off
END_IF;

// Manage cooling timer (protection lift)
CoolingTimer(IN := bOverheatProtection, PT := T#3s);
rTrigCoolingQ(CLK := CoolingTimer.Q);
IF rTrigCoolingQ.Q THEN
    bOverheatProtection := FALSE;
END_IF;

// Output statuses
OverheatProtection := bOverheatProtection;
HeaterStatus := HeaterOutput;

This seems correct. Need to consider edge cases:
- If sensor fault occurs while heater on: HeaterOutput becomes FALSE, HeaterOnTimer resets, no protection trigger. bOverheatProtection remains whatever it was. If protection was active, cooling timer continues. That's fine.
- If sensor fault clears, normal operation resumes, if temp low and not protected, heater turns on.
- If protection active and sensor fault occurs, HeaterOutput remains off anyway.
- If protection active and sensor fault clears, still protection until cooling timer expires, then normal.

We need to declare TON and R_TRIG instances. In ST, we can use standard function blocks. We should include VAR block with instances.

Also, check data types: TemperatureSensorInput REAL, SetTemperature REAL. Compare with < and >. Use 0.0 and 100.0.

Now, write the complete function block. We need to name the FB. The user didn't specify a name, but typical is something like "FB_TemperatureControl" or just "TemperatureControl". We can use "TemperatureControlFB". We'll define:

FUNCTION_BLOCK TemperatureControlFB

VAR_INPUT
    TemperatureSensorInput: REAL;
    SetTemperature: REAL;
END_VAR
VAR_OUTPUT
    CurrentTemperature: REAL;
    SetTempDisplay: REAL;
    HeaterStatus: BOOL;
    OverheatProtection: BOOL;
    HeaterOutput: BOOL;
END_VAR
VAR
    HeaterOnTimer: TON;
    CoolingTimer: TON;
    rTrigHeaterOnQ: R_TRIG;
    rTrigCoolingQ: R_TRIG;
    bOverheatProtection: BOOL := FALSE;
    bSensorFault: BOOL;
END_VAR

Then body as described.

We must ensure we call the functions correctly. In ST, function block instances are called with parentheses and parameter assignments. For timers, we need to call them each cycle with IN and PT. For edge triggers, call with CLK.

So:

HeaterOnTimer(IN := HeaterOutput, PT := T#5s);
rTrigHeaterOnQ(CLK := HeaterOnTimer.Q);
CoolingTimer(IN := bOverheatProtection, PT := T#3s);
rTrigCoolingQ(CLK := CoolingTimer.Q);

Note: The R_TRIG output Q is true for one cycle when CLK goes from false to true. The timer HeaterOnTimer.Q remains true as long as condition is true, so R_TRIG will detect the rising edge exactly when it first becomes true.

We need to ensure that when HeaterOutput is forced off due to protection trigger, the timer input becomes false immediately after we set HeaterOutput := FALSE in the same scan. However, HeaterOnTimer was already called with the old HeaterOutput (which was TRUE because it was turned on before). Then after its call, we set HeaterOutput FALSE. That means the timer's input during this scan was TRUE, and it might have just reached Q. The timer's Q becomes TRUE at the moment ET reaches PT. That rising edge is detected. Then we set HeaterOutput FALSE, but the timer has already processed with input TRUE. In the next scan, input will be FALSE, and timer will reset. That's fine. But is there a risk that the timer Q becomes TRUE in the same scan where we set HeaterOutput FALSE after the call? No, because the timer is called before we set HeaterOutput false. So the timer sees input TRUE for that scan, and if ET >= PT, Q becomes true. That's exactly when we want to trigger protection. Then we set output false, and next scan timer sees false and resets. So it's correct.

One nuance: The timer TON behavior: when IN is FALSE, Q is FALSE, ET is 0. When IN is TRUE, ET starts counting. When ET reaches PT, Q becomes TRUE and remains TRUE while IN is TRUE and ET >= PT. So if we set HeaterOutput false after Q became true, next scan Q will become false because IN is false. So R_TRIG will detect the rising edge on the scan where Q first becomes true, which is correct.

Now for CoolingTimer: It is called with bOverheatProtection as IN. When protection triggers, bOverheatProtection becomes true, so timer starts. After 3s, Q becomes true. R_TRIG detects that, and we set bOverheatProtection false. That will cause the timer input to become false next scan, resetting it. So correct.

We must also consider that if bOverheatProtection is true and then sensor fault occurs, we do not affect protection; it remains until cooling timer expires. So fine.

Now, write the final answer as a code block with the FB definition.

Check requirements: "displaying the current temperature, set temperature, heater status, and whether overheating protection is triggered." We have outputs for those. HeaterStatus = HeaterOutput, which shows whether heater is on/off.

Potential issue: When overheat protection is triggered, HeaterStatus becomes false (since output off), and OverheatProtection output becomes true. That's okay.

We should also include comments explaining logic.

